Inari for Debian
================

The package installs a single static binary with the web interface compiled
in. There is nothing else to deploy: no Node.js, no npm, no container runtime.

Files
-----

  /usr/bin/inari-server              the server
  /etc/inari/config.env              operator configuration (a dpkg conffile)
  /etc/inari/secret.env              session secret, generated at install time
  /usr/lib/systemd/system/inari.service

Getting started
---------------

The service is left stopped after installation, because it needs an endpoint
configured before it is of any use.

  1. Edit /etc/inari/config.env. At minimum set DEFAULT_S3_ENDPOINT, which is
     the address pre-filled on the connect screen. Users still supply their own
     credentials; the server holds none of its own.

  2. Start it:

       sudo systemctl enable --now inari

  3. Confirm:

       systemctl status inari
       curl -sf http://127.0.0.1:3000/healthz

Configuration errors are start-up failures rather than request-time surprises.
If the unit refuses to start, `journalctl -u inari` names the reason, and never
echoes a secret value.

The session secret
------------------

/etc/inari/secret.env is generated once, at install time, and is unique to the
host. It seals the cookie holding each user's S3 credentials, so changing it
signs everyone out. It is deliberately not a conffile: keeping it out of the
package means an upgrade never prompts about a file containing a secret.

Setting SESSION_SECRET in config.env overrides it, which is what to do when
several hosts must share sessions behind a load balancer.

Reverse proxy
-------------

The service listens on 127.0.0.1:3000 by default and speaks plain HTTP. Put a
TLS-terminating proxy in front of it before exposing it to anything.

The package intentionally does not configure one. An example nginx server block
is shipped with the source at deploy/nginx.example.conf; two settings in it are
load-bearing and easy to miss:

  * The proxy must forward the browser's Host header (`proxy_set_header Host
    $http_host`). Mutating requests are refused unless the Origin matches the
    Host, which is what blocks cross-site submission of a session carrying S3
    credentials. Forwarding the proxy's own upstream host makes every write
    fail with 403.

  * Usage and cleanup scans walk every object beneath their scope and can run
    for minutes. nginx's 60-second default read timeout cuts them off as a 504.

Leave SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE alone when a proxy terminates TLS. What decides it
is the browser's connection, not the proxy's connection to this service.

Serving under a URL prefix
--------------------------

Set BASE_PATH=/dashboard in config.env. The prefix is applied at run time, so
no rebuild is needed and the health endpoints stay at the root, outside the
prefix, where a monitoring check can reach them without knowing the mount
point.

Removing
--------

`apt remove` stops the service and leaves /etc/inari in place. `apt purge` also
removes the configuration and the generated secret.
